questions.question_text:
Which of the graphs $A$–$D$ could be the graph of $y=2^{-x}$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.9]
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,0)}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(1.6,0); \draw[->] (0,-1.4)--(0,1.8);
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-1.25:1.25,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.1*\x*\x-0.7});
\node at (-1.15,1.55) {$A$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,0)}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(1.6,0); \draw[->] (0,-1.4)--(0,1.8);
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-1.4:0.8,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\node at (-1.15,1.55) {$B$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-4)}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(1.6,0); \draw[->] (0,-1.4)--(0,1.8);
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-0.8:1.4,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(-\x*ln(2))});
\node at (-1.15,1.55) {$C$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-4)}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(1.6,0); \draw[->] (0,-1.4)--(0,1.8);
\draw[thick,blue,<->] (-1.2,-1.0)--(1.3,1.35);
\node at (-1.15,1.55) {$D$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which equation could produce the graph shown?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (-2.6,0)--(2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-2.05:2.05,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-\x*\x});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which equation matches the graph shown?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1)--(0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (-0.12,2)--(0.12,2) node[left] {$2$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-1.3:1.3,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2*\x*\x+2});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
